WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.510
- [Narrator] Most
skydivers jump off a plane

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.470
flying 3.8 kilometers above the ground.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.119
But imagine jumping off
something even higher

00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:13.090
like the International Space Station.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.741
Unless you have a super
suit like Tony Stark,

00:00:15.741 --> 00:00:18.150
it's not gonna end well.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.271
But let's pretend Iron Man lends you one.

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:22.990
Okay, ready?

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:26.219
Three, two, one, jump.

00:00:26.219 --> 00:00:28.500
Wait, what?

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.333
That's right.

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:31.010
You won't fall straight down.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.260
In fact it'll take you at least two

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.380
and half years before
you reach the surface.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.883
So what's going on?

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.770
Height isn't the main reason
your fall takes so long.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.708
In fact, if you fell
like a normal skydiver

00:00:50.708 --> 00:00:54.014
it would only take you about two hours.

00:00:54.014 --> 00:00:57.033
But the thing is, you
don't fall straight down,

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.660
you fall into orbit.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.530
The reason is speed.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.495
You see, the ISS might be called a station

00:01:04.495 --> 00:01:07.120
but it's hardly stationary.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.253
It's actually moving 12 times
faster than a jet fighter.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.920
If you shot anything
at that speed on Earth,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.966
by the time it was about to
hit the ground it would miss.

00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:22.601
In the same way, the ISS
isn't floating in space,

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:26.390
it's falling towards Earth and missing.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.780
And when you jump off the
ISS, you're initially moving

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.220
at the same speed.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.977
So you end up in orbit too.

00:01:32.977 --> 00:01:35.150
At least for a while.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.990
Now even though it's so
high up, the ISS is pushing

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.180
through a very thin atmosphere.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.281
So that friction against the
atmosphere slows it down.

00:01:44.281 --> 00:01:47.740
So the station fires
engines to maintain speed

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.010
and keep from crashing into the Earth.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.400
But sadly your super suit
doesn't come with engines

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.070
strapped to your feet.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.121
This has two consequences.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.895
First, it means you can't
maneuver and have to hope

00:02:00.895 --> 00:02:05.170
that any of those 13 thousand
chunks of space debris

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.624
don't empale you.

00:02:06.624 --> 00:02:10.682
Second, without rockets
to maintain your speed,

00:02:10.682 --> 00:02:14.030
it'll slow you down and
spiral toward Earth.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.769
But it won't be quick.

00:02:15.769 --> 00:02:19.967
The Chinese Space Station,
Tiongong-1 for example

00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:23.243
took about two years to fall out of orbit.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:25.907
On the ISS, you're higher up.

00:02:25.907 --> 00:02:28.812
So you'll take roughly
two and a half years.

00:02:28.812 --> 00:02:32.606
But once you strike the
atmosphere your long wait is over

00:02:32.606 --> 00:02:35.157
and it's go time.

00:02:35.157 --> 00:02:39.488
As you re-enter, you
have one goal, slow down.

00:02:39.488 --> 00:02:42.187
You're traveling at hyper-sonic speeds.

00:02:42.187 --> 00:02:47.187
So if you deployed a parachute
now, it'll shred to pieces.

00:02:47.291 --> 00:02:49.532
And that's not he only problem.

00:02:49.532 --> 00:02:53.260
Falling through the atmosphere
at such break-neck speeds

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.118
generates at least eight G's of force.

00:02:56.118 --> 00:02:59.690
That's eight times the
gravity you feel at sea level.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:02.680
And if you're falling feet first,

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.000
that'll push the blood
away from your brain

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.400
and toward your feet.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.360
So you'll probably pass out,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.990
unless you're one of those fighter pilots

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.373
who trained to withstand up to five G's.

00:03:13.373 --> 00:03:17.120
Now if you don't pass
out, you may worry about

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.983
the freezing temperatures up here.

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:22.450
But it turns out your suit's

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:24.759
more likely to melt than freeze.

00:03:24.759 --> 00:03:26.930
You know how you can warm your hands

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.640
by rubbing them together?

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.270
Now imagine your
super-suit rubbing against

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:34.800
air molecules in the
atmosphere at least six times

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.200
the speed of sound.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.077
You'll heat up to about
1,650 degrees Celsius.

00:03:40.077 --> 00:03:42.707
Hot enough to melt iron.

00:03:42.707 --> 00:03:47.130
In fact, the heat is so
intense, it strips electrons

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.880
from their atoms, forming
a pink plasma around you

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.229
that will ultimately destroy your suit.

00:03:53.229 --> 00:03:55.509
If that's not enough of a problem,

00:03:55.509 --> 00:03:57.803
the drag will rip off your limbs.

00:03:57.803 --> 00:04:01.890
But thankfully Tony Stark has your back

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:05.950
and somehow your super-suit
holds with you in tact.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:09.720
At 41 kilometers up, you've
now reached the world record

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.450
for highest skydive.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.620
In 2014, Alan Eustace wore
a pressurized spacesuit

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.100
as he rode a balloon up to this height.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.510
He broke the sound barrier on his way down

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.200
before deploying his parachute.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.500
And landed about 15
minutes after the drop.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.995
But you'll be falling
much faster than Eustace.

00:04:27.995 --> 00:04:31.013
About three times the speed of sound.

00:04:32.053 --> 00:04:34.827
So in reality you're not
going to slow down enough

00:04:34.827 --> 00:04:37.310
to safely deploy your chute.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.860
That's where Iron Man can
help us one last time.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.590
By one kilometer up, you've
reached the territory

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.652
of ordinary skydivers who don't
need fancy suits to survive.

00:04:47.652 --> 00:04:51.184
At this point, your parachute
can do what it does best,

00:04:51.184 --> 00:04:54.373
and it's finally time to land softly.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:57.643
What a ride.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.892
What sort of daring feat
would you want us to try next?

00:05:01.892 --> 00:05:04.100
Let us know in the comments below.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.313
And thanks for watching.

